# THE SIGNADOT COMMENT-DRIFT REVIEW: verse-dev's review half, pointed at example/app.
#
# One half of a two-file program. This file reads what has landed on signadot's master since it
# last looked, judges the Go comments against the tree as it stands, fixes what disagrees and
# delivers each fix as a branch; pr.tony beside it is what turns those branches into a pull
# request. Install both —
#
#     verse charter -a -f docs/working/signadot/comment-drift.tony
#     verse charter -a -f docs/working/signadot/pr.tony
#
# — this one first, because pr.tony reads it and a charter that reads an address nobody writes is
# refused.
#
# It is docs/working/comment-drift.tony's review, and the reasons in that file are not restated
# here: the unit of review is a RANGE reviewed against its far end, the cursor is patched and not
# derived, a finding is addressed by its symbol, and a fix resolves nothing until the review after
# it lands reads master and finds the comment true. What differs is the repository, and three
# things follow from that:
#
#   - THE DEFAULT BRANCH IS `master`. Every read of "the tip" is `git.head.signadot`, and the
#     environment's base is master.
#   - IT IS SEVERAL GO MODULES, not one. `common`, `sandboxes`, `playground/app` and the ones under
#     `infra` are each their own module, so a symbol is named from the REPOSITORY root
#     (`common/controllers.Reconcile`, not `controllers.Reconcile`) and `go doc` runs inside the
#     module that holds it. The prompt says so.
#   - `third_party` IS NOT THEIRS TO FIX. Vendored code drifts on somebody else's schedule, and a
#     comment fix there is overwritten on the next vendoring. Generated files (`*.pb.go`) likewise.
#     Both are excluded from the diff the reviewer is pointed at.
#
# ── SEEDING ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The cursor stands where the first range starts, so seed it at master's tip and the first range
# is empty — no backlog, whatever the mirror's window holds:
#
#     verse data signadot.comment-drift.at --put '{sha: <master tip>, stage: idle}'
#
# ── THE RUNNER ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `verse.runners.pool.code` needs an environment named `signadot-fix`: the `verse-fix` shape with
# the repository `https://github.com/example/app`, checkout name `signadot`, base `master`,
# and the one destination `auto: refs/heads/verse/auto/$sha/$task`. No `push` tool, for the reason
# verse-fix has none — a fix proposes and never lands, as a property of the environment.
at: signadot.comment-drift
reads: [ !verse-path [git] ]
---
# The cursor: one entity, patched by rules whose subject it is. It bounces idle → claimed → idle.
- name: cursor
  level: 10
  match:
    path: [signadot, comment-drift, at]

# One entity per range, keyed by its FAR end, which is what every downstream reader names.
- name: range
  level: 20
  match:
    path: [signadot, comment-drift, range]

# The ledger: one row per symbol, open or resolved.
- name: ledger
  level: 30
  match:
    path: [signadot, comment-drift, finding]
---
# 1. THE CLAIM. The tip is read here and written down as `covering`, so a commit landing while
# the review runs is neither covered nor skipped: rule 5 advances to what was CLAIMED. The read
# joins this trigger's reads, so master moving is what wakes it.
- name: claim
  stratum: cursor
  condition:
    subject:
      path: [signadot, comment-drift, at]
      value: !let
        let:
        - tip: !get-path
            at: [git, head, signadot]
            path: sha
        in:
          stage: idle
          sha: !not .[tip]
  action:
    patch:
      stage: claimed
      covering: !get-path
        at: [git, head, signadot]
        path: sha
---
# 2. …and the range it claimed becomes an entity, so the review has a subject of its own — and
# therefore a task id of its own. A task's id is (rule, subject); spawned on the cursor every
# review would share one id, and a spawn on an id that already completed returns the old answer.
- name: open-range
  stratum: cursor
  condition:
    subject:
      path: [signadot, comment-drift, at]
      value:
        stage: claimed
  action:
    derive:
    - path: [signadot, comment-drift, range]
      id: $subject.value.covering
      value:
        from: $subject.value.sha
        to: $subject.value.covering
---
# 3. The STANDING SET is read onto the range before the review, in its own firing: a spawn's
# strings bind against the payload that fired the action, so a rule that patched `outstanding`
# and spawned in one action would hand the task the payload from before its own write.
#
# `status: open` is also the human override — set a finding's status to anything else and it
# stops being handed to the reviewer.
- name: gather
  stratum: range
  condition:
    subject:
      path: [signadot, comment-drift, range, "*"]
      value: !not.has-path stage
  action:
    patch:
      stage: gathered
      outstanding: !list-paths
        at: [signadot, comment-drift, finding, "*"]
        value:
          status: open
---
# 4. …and the range is reviewed, once. `stage: gathered` is the latch, and all three terminal
# hooks write past it.
- name: review-range
  stratum: range
  condition:
    subject:
      path: [signadot, comment-drift, range, "*"]
      value:
        stage: gathered
  action:
    spawn:
      runner: verse.runners.pool.code
      environment: signadot-fix
      driver: llm
      grant: all
      vector: auto
      # The answer is a contract, not a request in the prose: an unparseable answer is no answer,
      # and no answer leaves the range in no state at all.
      answer:
        files: !irtype 0
        findings: !irtype []
      prompt: |
        The signadot repository is checked out at ./signadot with its full history, at $to.

        Review the RANGE $from..$to — everything that has landed on master since the last
        review — and answer with `at: $to`. The range is what you review; $to is the commit the
        tree stands at, and it is what your answer is a claim about. master can move while you
        work, so a reader must not have to assume which commit you meant.

        THE REPOSITORY IS SEVERAL GO MODULES: `common`, `sandboxes`, `playground/app`, and the
        ones under `infra` each have their own go.mod. A Go command runs inside the module that
        holds the package (`cd example/common && go doc ./controllers Reconcile`), and a symbol
        is named from the REPOSITORY root so that two modules cannot name the same thing:
        `common/controllers.Reconcile`, `sandboxes/pkg/router.(*Router).Route`. That identity
        survives the comment being rewritten and the declaration moving between files, which a
        line number does not.

        WHAT STILL STANDS. These are the findings the last review left open — claims that some
        Go symbol's comments disagree with its code:

        $outstanding

        A finding you do not mention stays open on its own and will be handed to you again, so
        leaving it out is correct and re-listing it unchanged costs tokens for no fact. What you
        answer with is what CHANGED: `status: resolved` for one that is right in the tree you are
        reading, and whatever this range broke.

        Check each carried finding against the TREE, not against your memory of the diff: a
        comment can be fixed by a commit that never touched the file it names, and the range is
        only the reason to look.

        WHAT CHANGED. The Go files the range touched, leaving out vendored and generated code,
        which are not this repository's to correct:

            git -C signadot diff --name-only $from..$to -- '*.go' ':(exclude)third_party' ':(exclude)*.pb.go'

        For each of those files, read what changed in it:

            git -C signadot diff $from..$to -- <path>

        and judge the comments on the declarations that changed, plus any comment the change
        made wrong elsewhere in the same file. A comment is STALE when it DISAGREES with the
        code it is attached to: a parameter that is gone, a rationale for a branch that was
        deleted, a "returns X" that returns Y, an algorithm described that was replaced under
        it. Terse, unfashionable and MISSING are not stale — staleness is a disagreement, not a
        gap.

        A range is not a commit, and that changes one thing: a comment broken by one commit and
        corrected by a later one in the SAME range is not a finding. What you are judging is the
        tree at $to, and the intermediate states are somebody else's history.

        FIX each stale one in place with write_file: rewrite the comment to say what the code now
        does, in the voice of the comments around it. Change nothing else — not the code, not the
        formatting, not a comment that is merely unlovely. Run no git command that writes: the
        working tree is the handoff, and delivery is the runner's.

        WHAT YOU DO NOT DO, because the working tree IS the deliverable and every file you leave
        behind is committed: do not write a test, do not run one, do not build, and do not add a
        file. You are judging whether a comment disagrees with the code beside it — never whether
        the code is right. A test you write to satisfy yourself lands in somebody's pull request
        as though a reviewer had asked for it.

        A FIX YOU JUST WROTE RESOLVES NOTHING. Your edits are in a working tree that becomes a
        branch, and a branch is a PROPOSAL — it is gathered into a pull request that somebody
        approves, and it can be declined, or superseded, or retired unmerged while master moves
        on. So a symbol you fixed is still `status: open`, because it is still wrong in the tree
        you reviewed, and you say where you proposed the fix: `proposedAt: $to`. The review that
        comes after the fix LANDS reads master, finds the comment accurate, and resolves it. That
        is what makes the ledger a record of MASTER rather than of what agents believe they did.

        A finding you are handed that already carries `proposedAt` and is STILL WRONG means that
        fix did not land. Fix it again and say `proposedAt: $to` for this attempt: the work is
        cheap and idempotent, where a comment nobody corrects is permanent.

        READ EACH THING ONCE. Re-reading a file you have read does not make a verdict truer, and
        the second read costs a turn the first one already bought. When every changed declaration
        and every carried finding has a verdict, answer.

        YOUR FINAL MESSAGE IS THE ANSWER — a JSON document, nothing before it and nothing after
        it. Every key and every string is quoted. Report an entry ONLY for a symbol that has
        something to say: a symbol you judged and found ACCURATE is not an entry, and an empty
        `findings` list is a real answer.

            {
              "files": 2,
              "findings": [
                {"symbol": "common/controllers.Reconcile",
                 "status": "open",
                 "says": "the comment says it requeues on conflict; it returns the error",
                 "at": "$to",
                 "proposedAt": "$to"}
              ]
            }
    onDone:
      derive:
      - path: [signadot, comment-drift, finding]
        value: !each
          over: $data.findings
          id: $item.symbol
          value:
            symbol: $item.symbol
            status: $item.status
            says: $item.says
            at: $item.at
            proposedAt: $item.proposedAt
      patch:
        stage: reviewed
        status: done
        report: $data
        err: !delete null
    onFailed:
      patch:
        stage: failed
        status: failed
        err: $err
    onCancelled:
      patch:
        stage: !delete null
        err: !delete null
---
# 5. …and the cursor steps, onto the range it claimed, once that range is done. A failed review
# leaves `status: failed`, so it is not in the set: the chain stops where it broke, visibly, and
# `verse data signadot.comment-drift.at --put '{stage: idle}'` is the retry, which by then covers
# everything since.
- name: advance
  stratum: cursor
  condition:
    subject:
      path: [signadot, comment-drift, at]
      value: !let
        let:
        - done: !list-paths
            at: [signadot, comment-drift, range, "*"]
            value:
              status: done
            path: to
        in:
          stage: claimed
          covering: !or .[done]
  action:
    patch:
      sha: $subject.value.covering
      covering: !delete null
      stage: idle
